mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Mr Kipmasai Mini Mart — Backend API\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis canister backs the Mr Kipmasai Mini Mart storefront. It stores a product\ncatalogue and shopper orders, and exposes a public read API for the catalogue,\nan administrator-only write API for products, and a per-shopper order API.\n\n## Authentication and identity\n\n- Catalogue reads (`listProducts`, `getProduct`, `listCategories`,\n  `searchProducts`) are open to anyone, including signed-out visitors.\n- `placeOrder` is callable by any visitor. The caller principal is recorded as\n  the order owner; an anonymous caller is recorded as the anonymous principal.\n- `listMyOrders` and `getMyOrder` return only orders owned by the caller\n  principal. A different signed-in user cannot read another shopper's orders.\n- `createProduct`, `updateProduct` and `deleteProduct` require an administrator.\n  A non-admin caller is rejected with a trap.\n\nThe app's frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at\n`/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding the\nuser's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal\nagainst that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`).\nSuch a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it\nexpires.\n\nRegistration gates role-based access. The first signed-in caller to invoke\n`_initialize_access_control` becomes the administrator; subsequent callers\nbecome regular users. A caller that never signed in through the app's own\nfrontend is unregistered even when it belongs to the app's owner, and a\nsigned-in caller derived against a different origin is a different principal\nthan the one the frontend registered. An unregistered caller invoking an\nadmin-guarded endpoint receives a trap (`Unauthorized: only administrators can\ncreate products`, and likewise for update and delete).\n\n## Units and encoding\n\n- All monetary amounts are integer **sen** (1 Malaysian Ringgit = 100 sen).\n  Display RM as `sen / 100`.\n- `createdAt` is a signed nanosecond timestamp (`Time.now()`).\n- Product ids and order ids are `Nat`. Order references are human-readable text\n  of the form `KM-<id>`.\n- `image` is an optional object-storage `ExternalBlob` reference; the frontend\n  resolves a display URL from it.\n- Optional values (`notes`, `image`) are Candid `opt`; absent is `null`.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\n- The catalogue is seeded on first initialisation with sample products across\n  Fresh Produce, Groceries, Household, Beverages, Snacks and Personal Care.\n- `placeOrder` is synchronous: it validates every line item, decrements stock,\n  and returns the created order in one call. There is no order status field and\n  no delivery tracking; an order is final once placed.\n- Reads are `query` calls and can be polled freely.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n- `placeOrder` is **not** idempotent: each successful call creates a new order\n  and decrements stock again. Do not retry a call whose result was not observed\n  without first checking `listMyOrders`.\n- `createProduct` is not idempotent: each call allocates a new id.\n- `updateProduct` and `deleteProduct` are idempotent in effect; `deleteProduct`\n  returns `false` when the product is already gone.\n\n## Errors and gotchas\n\n- `placeOrder` traps when the item list is empty, when a quantity is zero, when\n  a product id does not exist, or when stock is insufficient. A trap rolls back\n  the whole call, so no partial order or stock change is persisted.\n- `getProduct` and `getMyOrder` return `null` for a missing or unowned record\n  rather than trapping.\n- Admin endpoints trap for non-admin callers; the frontend should gate the admin\n  UI on the caller's role.\n";
  };
};
